-> T26 Phase 1: `{ }` with bare elements is a set literal, distinct from an
-> object literal (`{ name = expr }`). `=` pins a variable to the set as a
-> concrete resolved VALUE — the variable IS the set, not one of its elements.
p = { 1, 2, 3 }
assert p ∈ Set

-> Duplicates are removed — {1,1,2,2,2,3} and {1,2,3} are the same set.
q = { 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3 }
r = { 1, 2, 3 }
assert q = r

-> An empty `{}` is an empty OBJECT, never an empty set (no empty-set
-> literal exists — Null already covers "no value").
empty = {}
assert empty ∈ Object

-> Object literals (`name = expr` fields) are completely unaffected.
o = { name = "Ada", age = 36 }
assert o.name = "Ada"

-> `{ a }` (single bare identifier) is a one-element set containing a's
-> value, never shorthand for an object field.
a = 5
single = { a }
assert single ∈ Set

-> Sets can nest — an element can itself be a set (this is why sets must be
-> first-class values, not just an internal narrowing representation).
inner = { 1, 2 }
nested = { 1, 2, inner }
assert nested ∈ Set
